nai has warmed by about 0.1 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 0.7 °C in all, with the coldest days warming fastest, so cold extremes are becoming rarer. There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding fall, Mar–May tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 15% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about as large as a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 38 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Mar–May this warm would come about once every 7 years. Relative humidity has risen by about 0.6 points per decade, roughly 4 points since 1951. Rainfall shows no clear trend or El Niño link. For Mar–May 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is likely (about 90% chance), about 0.6 °C above the 1991–2020 average; a more humid season than normal is likely (about 75% chance), mostly because of the long-term trend rather than El Niño. The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":29.3521,"trend_per_decade":0.0947,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":0.7106,"enso_effect_per_degC":0.1517,"enso_p":0.0006,"confidence":"Strong","survives_fdr":true,"fraction_explained":0.152,"intrinsic_sd":0.3358,"effect_this_season":0.3557,"effect_ratio":1.0593,"size":"Dominant","size_typical_el_nino":"Substantial","once_every_n_years_without_el_nino":7,"expected_anomaly_2027":0.5995,"forecast_mean":29.9517,"forecast_sd":0.3641,"p_below":0.0199,"p_near":0.0961,"p_above":0.8841,"forecast_words":"Likely (88%): a warmer-than-normal season","within_record":false,"enso_effect_shrunk_per_degC":0.1406,"shrink_factor":0.9267,"effect_this_season_raw":0.3838,"trend_part_2027":0.2439,